{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["And the verse's saying <b>and when you refrain from vowing, there shall be no sin in you</b> may also be explained along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Gittin 7a): if a person sees that his assets are diminishing, he should give charity from them. And they further said (Ketuvot 66b): salt [withhold] a little of the money. Now, since a person's wealth is precious to him, he is not inclined to part with it. Therefore, the reasoning may occur to a person to be clever and vow a vow to HaShem — for through this he will create for himself a willingness to give from his assets. Therefore the word of the King comes and informs him of what arises from the concern of violating the vow, and of what arises from the concern of withholding the giving of charity according to what one obligates himself." "<b>And He said</b> regarding the postponement of the vow — which will cause a loss to occur in it [the vow] — and regarding the withholding of giving charity, He said ״וְכִי תֶחְדַּל לִנְדֹּר״: even though you come to withhold the giving of charity, which will cause a loss in your money — nevertheless, בְּךָ, in you, there shall surely be no sin. That is what it says: ״לֹא יִהְיֶה בְךָ חֵטְא״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:48.658737167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}